<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Okinawa Prefecture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Okinawa Prefecture is the southernmost prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, created in 1879 when the Meiji government annexed the Ryukyu Kingdom, deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-tai">Sho Tai</a> and abolished the Ryukyu Domain. The islands saw the bloody <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a> in 1945 and remained under US administration until their return to Japan in 1972. American military bases still occupy much of the main island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture.jpg" alt="Okinawa Prefecture" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naha_Okinawa_Japan_Shuri-Castle-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Okinawa Prefecture is the southernmost and westernmost prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It consists of three main island groups, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Islands</a>, the Sakishima Islands and the Daitō Islands, spread across a maritime zone about 1,000 kilometres from east to west and 400 kilometres from north to south. Of its 160 islands, 49 are inhabited. The largest and most populous is Okinawa Island, which holds the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a>. The land area is 2,281 square kilometres, and the climate is subtropical, with warm temperatures and high rainfall through the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human activity on the islands goes back a long way: the Yamashita Cave Man is dated to about 32,000 years ago and the Minatogawa Man to about 18,000 years ago. From the 12th century the islands entered the Gusuku period, when an agrarian society grew on the limestone plateaus. Local chieftains called Aji built fortified residences known as gusuku and expanded their influence through foreign trade. The later Ryukyu Kingdom was a trading hub that kept tribute relations with the Ming and Qing courts and retained its own statehood.</p><p>After the Meiji Restoration, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> abolished the domains and set up prefectures in 1871. The Mudan Incident of that year, in which the crew of a drifting Ryukyuan ship was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, became a pretext for Japan to assert control. In 1872 the kingdom was reclassified as the Ryukyu Domain, a step known as the Ryukyu Disposition, and in 1879 it became Okinawa Prefecture. The last king, Shō Tai, was moved to Tokyo. Ryukyuan opponents of the change petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> was asked in 1879–1880 to mediate, without result.</p><p>On 1 April 1945 the United States Army and Marine Corps landed on Okinawa with about 185,000 troops. They met fierce resistance and won on 22 June. About one third of the civilian population died in the fighting, and the dead of all nationalities are commemorated at the Cornerstone of Peace. The caves of the coral island served as refuges for civilians during the battle. The islands were then under American administration until 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Okinawan culture shows the influence of its historical trading partners, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its music centres on the sanshin, a three-stringed instrument related to the Chinese sanxian and ancestor of the Japanese shamisen, and on eisa, a drumming dance. Karate originated on the islands. Six Ryukyuan languages remain, mutually unintelligible with Japanese. Shuri Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusuku-sites-and-related-properties-of-the-kingdom-of-ryukyu">Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu</a> are on the UNESCO World Heritage List. The American bases cover about 10 per cent of the prefecture's land.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Okinawa Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1879 Matsuda Michiyuki arrived at Shuri with police and troops, the domain was abolished and replaced by <b>Okinawa Prefecture</b>, and Sho Tai was removed to Tokyo, where he was later made a marquis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 March 2012, after a public survey, the government of <b>Okinawa Prefecture</b> designated &quot;Tinsagu nu Hana&quot; as &quot;Okinawa Prefecture's favorite song&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tinsagu-nu-hana">Tinsagu nu Hana</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1879 the official Matsuda Michiyuki arrived with police and troops, abolished the domain and created <b>Okinawa Prefecture</b>; Sho Tai was moved to Tokyo.</blockquote><a href="/state/ryukyu-domain">Ryukyu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
